  "why": "B21, Java. Every call goes through a receiver whose type is written: an interface-typed field (lands on the interface member, D2), `this.backing` whose `name()` is inherited from the interface's own super-interface (found on the nearest declaring type, D4 — which needs `interface Store extends Named` as an IMPLEMENTS edge, missing before), a parameter, a typed local, `var v = new DbStore()`, a field inherited from the superclass, a parameter typed with a member type inherited from an implemented interface (`Listener`, declared inside `Store`), and an inner class calling through its enclosing class's field (D13). `Store` is reached through a wildcard import, so `Orders implements Store` is itself a case.",
